=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** iptables FORWARD chain on router6, specifically three DROP rules applied to all traffic entering via `eth2` (the `r6_r3` link connecting router6 to router3 — the WAN backbone).

**Mechanism:** Router6 sits between the mswitch2-side networks (172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24, where pc15–pc18 and laptop4–5 live) and the rest of the campus routing domain (router3, then router0, router1, router2, router5). Any packet arriving at router6 from router3 (entering via eth2) hits these FORWARD rules in order: (1) ACCEPT TCP from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1 port 80 — a rule that is dead on arrival because 172.18.51.1 has no host; (2) DROP all TCP via eth2; (3) DROP all UDP via eth2; (4) DROP all remaining protocols via eth2. Rule 4 has consumed 24 packets (active blocking confirmed). The net effect is that no traffic from the main campus network can transit router6 to reach the 172.18.51.x/172.18.50.x segment, and no reply traffic from those segments can return (because every return packet must re-enter router6 via eth2, where it is immediately dropped).

**Localization:** Router6, FORWARD chain, interface `eth2` (`r6_r3` link). Direct-IP pings from both laptop0 (172.16.20.10) and pc4 (172.16.10.4) to 172.18.51.15 timed out. The user's path to 172.16.10.x via router0 is intact (0% loss by IP); the "ban" they perceive is cross-area isolation to the 172.18.51.x side.

**Root cause:** Three injected iptables DROP rules on router6's FORWARD chain block all forwarded traffic ingressing on eth2, severing bidirectional connectivity between the mswitch2 networks and the rest of the campus.

**Fix:** On router6, delete the three DROP rules: `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`, `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP`, and `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP`. If the narrow HTTP exception was intentional, replace the non-existent destination (172.18.51.1) with the correct host IP before re-applying.
=== END ===